From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 07:54:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: exynos: exynos9820: Add CMU_AUD

Describe the clock controller of the audio block. Its window is the
CMU_AUD base of the vendor register map and its input is the audio CPU
clock CMU_TOP divides down.

The block sits in the AUD power domain, but the node does not name it
yet: the boot loader leaves the domain on, and until a driver in this
tree holds one of the audio clocks, runtime PM would switch the domain
off right after boot and take the whole block with it.

Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820.dtsi | 15 +++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820.dtsi
index ec828743d0b..b092f12e72a 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/exynos/exynos9820.dtsi
@@ -1052,6 +1052,21 @@ gpu: gpu@18500000 {
 			#cooling-cells = <2>;
 		};
 
+		/*
+		 * No power-domains yet: the boot loader leaves the AUD domain on
+		 * and nothing in this series holds a clock of the block, so runtime
+		 * PM would switch the domain off right after boot.
+		 */
+		cmu_aud: clock-controller@18c00000 {
+			compatible = "samsung,exynos9820-cmu-aud";
+			reg = <0x18c00000 0x8000>;
+			#clock-cells = <1>;
+
+			clocks = <&oscclk>,
+				 <&cmu_top CLK_DOUT_CMU_AUD_CPU>;
+			clock-names = "oscclk", "cpu";
+		};
+
 		pinctrl_aud: pinctrl@18c60000 {
 			compatible = "samsung,exynos9820-pinctrl";
 			reg = <0x18c60000 0x1000>;
